Networking in DevOps – Why It Matters ❓ 
•To deploy applications across cloud and on-prem environments.
•To troubleshoot network issues in production.
•To configure load balancers, DNS, firewalls, and proxies.
•To ensure secure communication between services (VPCs, VPNs, TLS, etc.).
•To manage networking in container orchestration tools like Kubernetes.

🚀 Core Networking Concepts:
•IP Addressing: Assign static/dynamic OR public/private IPs to servers or containers. 
•Subnets & CIDR: Divide larger networks securely and efficiently into smaller sub-network.
•Ports: Expose services (e.g., port 80 for HTTP, 443 for HTTPS).
•DNS: Resolve domain names for microservices.
•TCP/IP: The foundational communication protocol suite that enables devices to connect and exchange data over the internet
•HTTP/HTTPS: Protocols used for transferring web content—HTTPS adds encryption for secure communication.
•SSH: A secure protocol for remotely accessing and managing servers via encrypted terminal sessions.
•NAT & Gateways: Enable internet access inside private networks.   
•Firewalls & Security Groups: Control inbound/outbound traffic.   
•Load Balancers: Distribute traffic among multiple servers.  
•VPNs & VPCs: Secure communication in private environments. 
